The fork's own Zig 0.17 fixes for the Kitty graphics protocol.

OpenTUI's curated `ghostty-zig-0.17.patch` was written for a build that
DISABLES the Kitty graphics protocol (`-Dvt-features=-kitty-graphics,
-glyph-protocol`), so the kitty-graphics code path was never semantically
analyzed and its Zig 0.17 breakages were never fixed. The fork ENABLES
the protocol (the embedded terminal renders Kitty images), which exposes
them.

The concrete breakage: Zig 0.17 gave `std.ArrayList` a `pointer_stability`
field, so the struct literal that adopts an owned slice

    managed = .{ .items = data, .capacity = data.len };

no longer compiles ("missing struct field: pointer_stability"). The
curated patch fixed the two neighbouring literals but not this one.

Every form below is Ghostty's own Zig 0.17 migration, so the vendored
kitty-graphics closure matches Ghostty's own 0.17 tree. Only API
migrations are taken; Ghostty's unrelated feature changes (its macOS
shared-memory name policy, its extra tests) are deliberately left out.

This patch is applied AFTER `ghostty-zig-0.17.patch`.

diff --git a/src/terminal/c/kitty_graphics.zig b/src/terminal/c/kitty_graphics.zig
index 0b78537..c5df7d3 100644
--- a/src/terminal/c/kitty_graphics.zig
+++ b/src/terminal/c/kitty_graphics.zig
@@ -305,7 +305,7 @@ pub fn placement_iterator_set(
     if (comptime !build_options.kitty_graphics) return .no_value;
 
     if (comptime std.debug.runtime_safety) {
-        _ = std.enums.fromInt(PlacementIteratorOption, @intFromEnum(option)) orelse {
+        _ = std.enums.fromInt(PlacementIteratorOption, @backingInt(option)) orelse {
             return .invalid_value;
         };
     }
diff --git a/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_command.zig b/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_command.zig
index e57ad17..d82f4da 100644
--- a/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_command.zig
+++ b/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_command.zig
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@ const log = std.log.scoped(.kitty_gfx);
 /// A `[52]?u32` table is 416 bytes versus 216 bytes for this layout, and
 /// its larger copies erased the smaller lookup code.
 /// `std.EnumMap` added optional-return lowering,
-/// `std.StaticBitSet(52)` kept the same size but generated larger code
+/// `std.bit_set.Static(52)` kept the same size but generated larger code
 /// for the packed mask. Passing the table by pointer removed the copies
 /// but did not improve end-to-end throughput. Keep this representation
 /// unless a new benchmark shows an improvement in the zig compilers codegen.
diff --git a/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_exec.zig b/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_exec.zig
index b1b968b..c3efecb 100644
--- a/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_exec.zig
+++ b/src/terminal/kitty/graphics_exec.zig
@@ -2405,7 +2405,7 @@ test "kittygfx relative placement chain depth limit" {
         var buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
         const cmd = try command.Parser.parseString(
             alloc,
-            try std.fmt.bufPrint(&buf, "a=p,i=1,p={},P=1,Q={}", .{ id, id - 1 }),
+            try std.mem.print(&buf, "a=p,i=1,p={},P=1,Q={}", .{ id, id - 1 }),
         );
         defer cmd.deinit(alloc);
         try testing.expect(execute(io, alloc, &t, &cmd).?.ok());
